---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const timeline = [
  {
    date: "2018",
    datetime: "2018",
    title: "Diagnosis and surgery",
    description: "Diagnosed with breast cancer; undergoes a single mastectomy.",
  },
  {
    date: "2018–2022",
    datetime: "2018/2022",
    title: "Living without proper fit",
    description:
      "Lives with an ill-fitting prosthesis; introduced to a Mombasa missionary who teaches her what proper fit means.",
    range: true,
  },
  {
    date: "2023",
    datetime: "2023",
    title: "A trip to Raleigh",
    description:
      "Travels to Raleigh, NC with Nneka Hay; a store owner agrees to gather donations. God's Girls Foundation is born.",
  },
  {
    date: "2026",
    datetime: "2026-07-14",
    title: "In memory",
    description: "Pam passes away on July 14, 2026.",
    final: true,
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="About Pam Dreda Oyola — God's Girls Foundation"
  description="The story of Pam Dreda Oyola, founder of God's Girls Foundation, and why she started it."
>
  <section class="about-opening" aria-labelledby="about-name">
    <span class="about-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <h1 id="about-name" class="about-name">Pam Dreda Oyola</h1>
    <p class="about-dates">– 2026</p>
    <p class="about-role">Founder, God's Girls Foundation</p>
  </section>

  <section class="about-portrait" aria-labelledby="about-portrait-title">
    <figure class="founder-photo">
      <img
        src="/assets/founder-photo.jpg"
        alt="Portrait of Pam Dreda Oyola"
        width="1178"
        height="1178"
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </figure>
    <div class="about-portrait-copy">
      <h2 id="about-portrait-title" class="about-portrait-label">Introduction</h2>
      <p>
        Pam started God's Girls Foundation because she knew, firsthand, what it felt like to walk without dignity after surviving breast cancer. This is her story, in the words of the people who carry her mission forward.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="about-narrative" aria-labelledby="about-narrative-title">
    <div class="about-narrative-head">
      <span class="about-narrative-rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="about-narrative-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <h2 id="about-narrative-title">Her story</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="about-narrative-body">
      <p>
        In 2018, I was diagnosed with breast cancer and a single mastectomy procedure was done. After recovery, sometimes I walked bare without a prosthesis or anything to help. After some time a dear friend gifted me one which was knitted from an organization that brought women together and knitted them. This was the best gift ever because it came in handy.
      </p>
      <p>
        It was not that comfortable because the wool put inside shrank over time, making the whole thing small. This created the embarrassing issue of one breast being bigger than the other. It was uncomfortable and in most occasions, I would run to the bathroom to adjust it right in the middle of a church service or meeting. It was more problematic if I was the minister for the service.
      </p>
      <p>
        I became self conscious as I felt people noticed. It was always an awkward moment for me. I saw many women in church, at the stores, in hospitals and Drs offices in the same predicament. I remembered that my late mum struggled with the same thing. She too battled breast cancer. She would use old crampled clothes inside her bra. More often than not, it was not a pretty sight. Her confidence was crashed as it made everyone aware of her state, even if it was unspoken. At the time of my diagnosis, I was not even aware of where I could get breast prosthesis. I never saw a shop that sold them nor the bras to accommodate them. I was told of one shop in town but never tried locating it because the prices were so high.
      </p>
      <p>
        My relief came when I was introduced to a dear lady from the US. She, a breast cancer survivor herself, was a missionary in Mombasa. Each year, she would open her yard to sell prosthesis, bras, swimming costumes, and anything else that a breast cancer survivor would use. I learned a lot from her including the essential elements of a good bra post mastectomy:
      </p>
      <ul class="about-narrative-list">
        <li>soft seams</li>
        <li>wide underband that sits flat on your chest between the cups</li>
        <li>deep front and side panels</li>
        <li>full cups</li>
        <li>wide, elasticised, adjustable straps</li>
        <li>no underwire.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        I saved up and purchased supplies from her for a couple of years. These supplies together with the education I received from her significantly impacted my life. I felt more confident and comfortable in front of crowds, and gladly introduced other women who had endured mastectomy surgery to her. The joy on their faces after being fitted with a bra and having their dignity restored, always blessed my heart.
      </p>
      <p>
        Unfortunately, she had a recurrence of cancer and passed away. This really crushed a number of ladies who depended on her. These ladies continue to search for a supplier of these necessary supplies as there is such a shortage in Kenya. When available, they are often unaffordable for most women. May women are still forced to use rags as makeshift prosthesis. This makes them easy to spot in public as their best look uneven and misshapen, causing embarrassment and shame.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is for these reasons that I felt the need to help in every way I can. After going through treatment last year (2023), my friend and sister Nneka Hay took me to a mastectomy supply store in Raleigh, North Carolina to purchase new supplies. She approached the store owner and explained the predicament of many women back in Kenya. God gave us favor with the store owner and she decided to help gather some donations for us, to restore dignity to the lives of these women.
      </p>
      <p>
        The idea of a foundation was birthed in that moment. I know God will touch people to give towards this noble cause to help women who are going through this hurdle and together you will help us to Inspire mastectomy survivors to walk with confidence and dignity just as our mission states.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="about-timeline" aria-labelledby="about-timeline-title">
    <h2 id="about-timeline-title" class="visually-hidden">Timeline of Pam's story and the foundation's origin</h2>
    <ol class="story-path story-path--four">
      {timeline.map((item) => (
        <li>
          <time
            datetime={item.datetime}
            class:list={[{ "about-range-mark": item.range, "about-final-mark": item.final }]}
          >
            {item.range ? (
              <>
                2018–
                <br />
                2022
              </>
            ) : (
              item.date
            )}
          </time>
          <div>
            <h3>{item.title}</h3>
            <p>{item.description}</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="about-tribute" aria-labelledby="about-tribute-line">
    <h2 id="about-tribute-line" class="about-tribute-line">Carry Her Mission Forward</h2>
    <p class="about-tribute-body">
      Pam didn't set out to build an institution. She set out to make sure no woman has to hide her dignity again. God's Girls Foundation continues that work in her name.
    </p>
    <div class="about-tribute-cta">
      <button type="button" class="primary-action" disabled aria-disabled="true">Donate in her memory</button>
      <p class="about-tribute-note">Donation link coming soon.</p>
      <form class="notify-form" aria-disabled="true">
        <label for="notify-email-about" class="visually-hidden">Email address</label>
        <input id="notify-email-about" type="email" placeholder="you@example.com" disabled />
        <button type="submit" disabled>Notify me</button>
      </form>
      <p class="notify-note">Email signup coming soon.</p>
    </div>
    <noscript>
      <style>
        .about-tribute-cta {
          opacity: 1;
          transform: none;
        }
      </style>
    </noscript>
  </section>
</Layout>

<script>
  const cta = document.querySelector(".about-tribute-cta");

  if (cta) {
    if ("IntersectionObserver" in window) {
      const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
        (entries, obs) => {
          for (const entry of entries) {
            if (entry.isIntersecting) {
              cta.classList.add("is-visible");
              obs.unobserve(entry.target);
            }
          }
        },
        { threshold: 0.2 },
      );
      observer.observe(cta);
    } else {
      cta.classList.add("is-visible");
    }
  }
</script>
